/-!
# The Artin braid group

The braid group `B n` on `n` strands is the group generated by the elementary braids
`σ 0, …, σ (n - 2)`, where `σ i` crosses strand `i` over strand `i + 1`, subject to

* `σ i * σ j = σ j * σ i` when the two crossings are disjoint, that is `|i - j| ≥ 2`, and
* `σ i * σ j * σ i = σ j * σ i * σ j` when they share a strand, that is `|i - j| = 1`.

These are exactly the Artin-Tits relations of the Coxeter matrix of type `A`, so
`TauCeti.BraidGroup n` is defined as `TauCeti.ArtinGroup (CoxeterMatrix.A (n - 1))` and inherits
the presentation and exponent-sum homomorphism proved there. This file restates the universal
property in the classical index form and adds the type-`A` bookkeeping: the two relations in their
classical index form, and the *permutation* homomorphism `B n →* Equiv.Perm (Fin n)` recording
where a braid sends its strands, which is what makes the presentation visibly non-degenerate.

Indexing follows the classical convention: `BraidGroup n` is the group on `n` strands, so its
generators are indexed by `Fin (n - 1)` and it acts on the `n` strands `Fin n`. In particular
`BraidGroup 0` and `BraidGroup 1` are trivial.

Two theorems keep the presentation honest. `TauCeti.BraidGroup.permHom_surjective` says that every
permutation of the strands is realised by a braid, and
`TauCeti.BraidGroup.not_commute_sigma` says that two generators sharing a strand do *not*
commute, so `BraidGroup n` is nonabelian as soon as `n ≥ 3`. Together with
`TauCeti.BraidGroup.sigma_ne_one` and `TauCeti.BraidGroup.orderOf_sigma` (each generator is
nontrivial and has infinite order), they rule out the collapse that a mis-stated relator set would
cause.

## Main definitions

* `TauCeti.BraidGroup n`: the braid group on `n` strands.
* `TauCeti.BraidGroup.sigma i`: the elementary braid crossing strands `i` and `i + 1`.
* `TauCeti.BraidGroup.lift`: the universal property in terms of the classical braid relations,
  with computation rule `lift_sigma` and sigma-phrased extensionality theorem `hom_ext`.
* `TauCeti.BraidGroup.strand` and `TauCeti.BraidGroup.strandSucc`: the two strands that `sigma i`
  crosses, as elements of `Fin n`.
* `TauCeti.BraidGroup.transposition i`: the transposition of those two strands.
* `TauCeti.BraidGroup.strandIncl`: the homomorphism adding one uncrossed strand,
  `BraidGroup (n + 1) →* BraidGroup (n + 2)`.
* `TauCeti.BraidGroup.castAdd` and `TauCeti.BraidGroup.natAdd`: the homomorphisms placing a braid
  on the bottom or the top strands of a larger braid group, the other strands uncrossed.
* `TauCeti.BraidGroup.permHom n`: the underlying-permutation homomorphism.
* `TauCeti.BraidGroup.pureSubgroup n`: the pure braid group, its kernel.
* `TauCeti.BraidGroup.quotientPureSubgroupEquiv n`: the braid group modulo the pure braids is the
  symmetric group on the strands.

## Main results

* `TauCeti.BraidGroup.sigma_mul_sigma_comm` and `TauCeti.BraidGroup.sigma_braid`: the two defining
  relations, in their classical index form.
* `TauCeti.BraidGroup.closure_range_sigma` and `TauCeti.BraidGroup.sigma_induction_on`:
  generation by the elementary braids and its induction principle.
* `TauCeti.BraidGroup.permHom_surjective`: every permutation of the strands underlies a braid.
* `TauCeti.BraidGroup.permHom_strandIncl_castSucc` and
  `TauCeti.BraidGroup.permHom_strandIncl_last`: adding an uncrossed strand permutes the old
  strands as before and fixes the new one.
* `TauCeti.BraidGroup.permHom_sigma_last`: the last elementary braid transposes the last two
  strands.
* `TauCeti.BraidGroup.sign_permHom`: the sign of the strand permutation of a braid is `(-1)` to
  its exponent sum.
* `TauCeti.BraidGroup.exponentParity_surjective` and
  `TauCeti.BraidGroup.index_ker_exponentParity`: from two strands on, the exponent parity is onto
  the group of order two.
* `TauCeti.BraidGroup.exponentSum_bijective_two`: the exponent sum identifies `B 2` with `ℤ`.
* `TauCeti.BraidGroup.index_pureSubgroup`: the pure braid group has index `n !`.
* `TauCeti.BraidGroup.strandIncl_mem_pureSubgroup` and
  `TauCeti.BraidGroup.comap_strandIncl_pureSubgroup`: adding an uncrossed strand neither creates
  nor destroys pure braids.
* `TauCeti.BraidGroup.not_commute_sigma` and `TauCeti.BraidGroup.exists_not_commute`: generators
  sharing a strand do not commute, so `BraidGroup n` is nonabelian for `n ≥ 3`.
* `TauCeti.BraidGroup.revPerm_mul_transposition` and
  `TauCeti.BraidGroup.revPerm_mul_transposition_mul_revPerm`: conjugation by `Fin.revPerm`
  reverses the adjacent transpositions.
* `TauCeti.BraidGroup.sigma_zpow_mem_pureSubgroup_iff` and
  `TauCeti.BraidGroup.sigma_pow_mem_pureSubgroup_iff`: precisely the even integer and natural
  powers of a generator are pure braids; in particular, the generator is not pure and its square
  is pure.

## References

* E. Artin, *Theorie der Zöpfe*, Abh. Math. Sem. Univ. Hamburg 4 (1925), 47-72.
* W. B. R. Lickorish, *An Introduction to Knot Theory*, Springer GTM 175 (1997), Chapter 1
  (printed p. 10).
* J. Birman, *Braids, Links, and Mapping Class Groups*, Annals of Mathematics Studies 82 (1974),
  Chapter 1.

In knot theory, braid closures present knots and links, and the closure of a braid word together
with Markov's theorem relates this presentation to knot diagrams.
-/

public section

namespace TauCeti

/-- The Artin braid group on `n` strands: the Artin-Tits group of the Coxeter matrix of type
`A (n - 1)`. This is motivated by the classical identification of the corresponding Coxeter group
with the symmetric group on the `n` strands, which is not formalized here; `permHom` below is
constructed directly from adjacent transpositions. -/
abbrev BraidGroup (n : ℕ) : Type := ArtinGroup (CoxeterMatrix.A (n - 1))

namespace BraidGroup

variable {n : ℕ}

/-- The elementary braid `σ i`, crossing strand `i` over strand `i + 1`. -/
def sigma (i : Fin (n - 1)) : BraidGroup n := ArtinGroup.gen (CoxeterMatrix.A (n - 1)) i

-- The definitions in this namespace keep their bodies sealed, and the characteristic lemmas
-- below are their whole public interface. Those lemmas are proved by `(rfl)` rather than `rfl`
-- because a theorem proved by a syntactic `rfl` is inferred to be `@[defeq]`, and an exported
-- `@[defeq]` theorem forces every definition it unfolds to be `@[expose]`d.

/-- An elementary braid is a standard Artin-Tits generator. This is the bridge that carries the
universal property `TauCeti.ArtinGroup.lift` and its computation rule over to `BraidGroup`. -/
theorem sigma_eq_gen (i : Fin (n - 1)) :
    sigma i = ArtinGroup.gen (CoxeterMatrix.A (n - 1)) i :=
  (rfl)

/-- Transport along an equality of strand counts sends each generator to the same crossing. -/
theorem cast_sigma {k : ℕ} (h : n = k) (i : Fin (n - 1)) :
    MulEquiv.cast (M := BraidGroup) h (sigma i) =
      sigma (Fin.cast (congrArg (· - 1) h) i) := by
  subst k
  simp [MulEquiv.cast_apply]

/-- Disjoint crossings commute. -/
theorem sigma_mul_sigma_comm {i j : Fin (n - 1)} (h : (i : ℕ) + 2 ≤ j ∨ (j : ℕ) + 2 ≤ i) :
    sigma i * sigma j = sigma j * sigma i := by
  simpa only [sigma_eq_gen] using ArtinGroup.gen_mul_gen_comm _ (coxeterMatrixA_eq_two h)

/-- Crossings sharing a strand satisfy the braid relation. -/
theorem sigma_braid {i j : Fin (n - 1)} (h : (i : ℕ) + 1 = j ∨ (j : ℕ) + 1 = i) :
    sigma i * sigma j * sigma i = sigma j * sigma i * sigma j := by
  simpa only [sigma_eq_gen] using ArtinGroup.gen_braid _ (coxeterMatrixA_eq_three h)

/-- Every elementary braid is nontrivial. -/
@[simp]
theorem sigma_ne_one (i : Fin (n - 1)) : sigma i ≠ 1 := by
  simpa only [sigma_eq_gen] using ArtinGroup.gen_ne_one _ i

/-- Every elementary braid has infinite order. -/
@[simp]
theorem orderOf_sigma (i : Fin (n - 1)) : orderOf (sigma i) = 0 := by
  simpa only [sigma_eq_gen] using ArtinGroup.orderOf_gen _ i

/-- A power of an elementary braid is trivial only for the exponent `0`. -/
@[simp]
theorem sigma_zpow_eq_one_iff (i : Fin (n - 1)) (k : ℤ) :
    sigma i ^ k = 1 ↔ k = 0 := by
  simpa only [sigma_eq_gen] using ArtinGroup.gen_zpow_eq_one_iff _ i k

/-- A natural-number power of an elementary braid is trivial only for the exponent `0`. -/
@[simp]
theorem sigma_pow_eq_one_iff (i : Fin (n - 1)) (k : ℕ) :
    sigma i ^ k = 1 ↔ k = 0 := by
  simpa only [sigma_eq_gen] using ArtinGroup.gen_pow_eq_one_iff _ i k

/-- The exponent sum of an elementary braid is one. -/
@[simp]
theorem exponentSum_sigma (i : Fin (n - 1)) :
    ArtinGroup.exponentSum (CoxeterMatrix.A (n - 1)) (sigma i) = Multiplicative.ofAdd 1 := by
  simpa only [sigma_eq_gen] using ArtinGroup.exponentSum_gen _ i

/-- The elementary braids generate the braid group. -/
@[simp]
theorem closure_range_sigma : Subgroup.closure (Set.range (sigma (n := n))) = ⊤ := by
  have hsigma : (sigma (n := n)) = ArtinGroup.gen (CoxeterMatrix.A (n - 1)) := by
    funext i
    exact sigma_eq_gen i
  rw [hsigma]
  exact ArtinGroup.closure_range_gen (CoxeterMatrix.A (n - 1))

/-- To prove a predicate for every braid, it suffices to prove it for the identity and elementary
braids and show that it is preserved by multiplication and inverse. -/
theorem sigma_induction_on {p : BraidGroup n → Prop} (b : BraidGroup n)
    (sigma : ∀ i : Fin (n - 1), p (BraidGroup.sigma i)) (one : p 1)
    (mul : ∀ b b' : BraidGroup n, p b → p b' → p (b * b'))
    (inv : ∀ b : BraidGroup n, p b → p b⁻¹) : p b := by
  apply ArtinGroup.gen_induction_on _ b
  · intro i
    simpa only [sigma_eq_gen] using sigma i
  · exact one
  · exact mul
  · exact inv

/-- The universal property of the braid-group presentation: a family satisfying distant
commutation and the adjacent braid relation extends to a homomorphism from `BraidGroup n`. -/
def lift {G : Type*} [Group G] (f : Fin (n - 1) → G)
    (hcomm : ∀ {i j : Fin (n - 1)},
      (i : ℕ) + 2 ≤ j ∨ (j : ℕ) + 2 ≤ i → f i * f j = f j * f i)
    (hbraid : ∀ {i j : Fin (n - 1)}, (i : ℕ) + 1 = j ∨ (j : ℕ) + 1 = i →
      f i * f j * f i = f j * f i * f j) :
    BraidGroup n →* G :=
  ArtinGroup.lift _ f <| by
    intro i j
    rcases eq_or_ne i j with rfl | hij
    · rfl
    have hv : (i : ℕ) ≠ (j : ℕ) := fun h ↦ hij (Fin.ext h)
    by_cases hadj : (i : ℕ) + 1 = j ∨ (j : ℕ) + 1 = i
    · rw [CoxeterSystem.braidWord, coxeterMatrixA_eq_three hadj,
        CoxeterSystem.braidWord, coxeterMatrixA_eq_three hadj.symm,
        prod_map_alternatingWord_three, prod_map_alternatingWord_three]
      exact (hbraid hadj).symm
    · have h2 : (i : ℕ) + 2 ≤ j ∨ (j : ℕ) + 2 ≤ i := by omega
      rw [CoxeterSystem.braidWord, coxeterMatrixA_eq_two h2,
        CoxeterSystem.braidWord, coxeterMatrixA_eq_two h2.symm,
        prod_map_alternatingWord_two, prod_map_alternatingWord_two]
      exact hcomm h2

/-- The homomorphism constructed by `BraidGroup.lift` takes each elementary braid to its
prescribed value. -/
@[simp]
theorem lift_sigma {G : Type*} [Group G] (f : Fin (n - 1) → G) (hcomm) (hbraid)
    (i : Fin (n - 1)) : lift f hcomm hbraid (sigma i) = f i := by
  simpa only [lift, sigma_eq_gen] using ArtinGroup.lift_gen _ f _ i

/-- Two homomorphisms from a braid group are equal if they agree on every elementary braid. The
generic `ArtinGroup.hom_ext` remains the registered extensionality theorem for this abbrev. -/
theorem hom_ext {G : Type*} [Group G] {f g : BraidGroup n →* G}
    (h : ∀ i, f (sigma i) = g (sigma i)) : f = g :=
  ArtinGroup.hom_ext _ fun i ↦ by simpa only [sigma_eq_gen] using h i

/-- On at most one strand there are no elementary braids, so every braid is trivial. -/
theorem eq_one_of_le_one (hn : n ≤ 1) (b : BraidGroup n) : b = 1 := by
  have h : MonoidHom.id (BraidGroup n) = 1 := hom_ext fun i ↦ absurd i.isLt (by omega)
  exact DFunLike.congr_fun h b

/-- The lower of the two strands crossed by `sigma i`. -/
def strand (i : Fin (n - 1)) : Fin n := ⟨i, by have := i.isLt; omega⟩

/-- The upper of the two strands crossed by `sigma i`. -/
def strandSucc (i : Fin (n - 1)) : Fin n := ⟨i + 1, by have := i.isLt; omega⟩

@[simp] theorem val_strand (i : Fin (n - 1)) : (strand i : ℕ) = i := (rfl)

@[simp] theorem val_strandSucc (i : Fin (n - 1)) : (strandSucc i : ℕ) = i + 1 := (rfl)

/-- Reversing the crossing index sends the lower crossed strand to the reversal of the upper
crossed strand. -/
@[simp]
theorem strand_rev (i : Fin (n - 1)) : strand i.rev = (strandSucc i).rev := by
  ext
  simp only [val_strand, val_strandSucc, Fin.val_rev]
  omega

/-- Reversing the crossing index sends the upper crossed strand to the reversal of the lower
crossed strand. -/
@[simp]
theorem strandSucc_rev (i : Fin (n - 1)) : strandSucc i.rev = (strand i).rev := by
  ext
  simp only [val_strand, val_strandSucc, Fin.val_rev]
  omega

/-- Adding `1` to the lower strand crossed by `sigma i` gives the upper crossed strand. -/
@[simp]
theorem strand_add_one [NeZero n] (i : Fin (n - 1)) : strand i + 1 = strandSucc i := by
  ext
  have hi := i.isLt
  simp only [Fin.val_add, val_strand, val_strandSucc, Fin.val_one',
    Nat.mod_eq_of_lt (show 1 < n by omega), Nat.mod_eq_of_lt (show (i : ℕ) + 1 < n by omega)]

/-- Rotating the strand indices cyclically sends the lower strand crossed by `sigma i` to the upper
one. Not `@[simp]` because `finRotate_apply` simplifies the left-hand side to `strand i + 1`, which
`strand_add_one` then simplifies to `strandSucc i`. -/
theorem finRotate_strand (i : Fin (n - 1)) : finRotate n (strand i) = strandSucc i := by
  have := (strand i).neZero
  rw [finRotate_apply, strand_add_one]

/-- The two strands crossed by an elementary braid are distinct. -/
theorem strand_ne_strandSucc (i : Fin (n - 1)) : strand i ≠ strandSucc i := by
  simp only [ne_eq, Fin.ext_iff, val_strand, val_strandSucc]
  omega

/-- Distinct braid indices have distinct upper strands. -/
theorem strandSucc_ne_strandSucc {i j : Fin (n - 1)} (h : i ≠ j) :
    strandSucc i ≠ strandSucc j := by
  simp only [ne_eq, Fin.ext_iff, val_strandSucc]
  exact fun hij ↦ h (Fin.ext (by omega))

/-- If `j` follows `i`, the lower strand of `i` differs from the upper strand of `j`. -/
theorem strand_ne_strandSucc_of_succ {i j : Fin (n - 1)} (h : (i : ℕ) + 1 = j) :
    strand i ≠ strandSucc j := by
  simp only [ne_eq, Fin.ext_iff, val_strand, val_strandSucc]
  omega

/-- If `j` follows `i`, its lower strand is the upper strand of `i`. -/
theorem strand_eq_strandSucc_of_succ {i j : Fin (n - 1)} (h : (i : ℕ) + 1 = j) :
    strand j = strandSucc i := by
  apply Fin.ext
  simpa only [val_strand, val_strandSucc] using h.symm

/-- Elementary braids on disjoint pairs of strands, with `i + 2 ≤ j` or `j + 2 ≤ i`, cross four
distinct strands. -/
theorem nodup_strand_strandSucc_strand_strandSucc {i j : Fin (n - 1)}
    (h : (i : ℕ) + 2 ≤ j ∨ (j : ℕ) + 2 ≤ i) :
    [strand i, strandSucc i, strand j, strandSucc j].Nodup := by
  simp only [List.nodup_cons, List.mem_cons, List.not_mem_nil, List.nodup_nil, and_true,
    not_or, not_false_eq_true, Fin.ext_iff, val_strand, val_strandSucc]
  omega

/-- The transposition of the two strands crossed by `sigma i`. -/
def transposition (i : Fin (n - 1)) : Equiv.Perm (Fin n) :=
  Equiv.swap (strand i) (strandSucc i)

/-- The defining equation of `TauCeti.BraidGroup.transposition`. -/
theorem transposition_eq_swap (i : Fin (n - 1)) :
    transposition i = Equiv.swap (strand i) (strandSucc i) :=
  (rfl)

/-- An adjacent transposition sends the lower strand it crosses to the upper one. -/
@[simp]
theorem transposition_apply_strand (i : Fin (n - 1)) : transposition i (strand i) = strandSucc i :=
  Equiv.swap_apply_left _ _

/-- An adjacent transposition sends the upper strand it crosses to the lower one. -/
@[simp]
theorem transposition_apply_strandSucc (i : Fin (n - 1)) :
    transposition i (strandSucc i) = strand i :=
  Equiv.swap_apply_right _ _

/-- The value of an adjacent transposition, on the underlying natural numbers. -/
theorem val_transposition_apply (i : Fin (n - 1)) (x : Fin n) :
    (transposition i x : ℕ) =
      if (x : ℕ) = i then (i : ℕ) + 1 else if (x : ℕ) = (i : ℕ) + 1 then (i : ℕ) else (x : ℕ) := by
  rw [transposition_eq_swap, Equiv.swap_apply_def]
  split_ifs <;> simp_all [Fin.ext_iff]

/-- The inverse equivalence of an adjacent transposition is itself. -/
@[simp]
theorem symm_transposition (i : Fin (n - 1)) :
    (transposition i).symm = transposition i := by
  rw [transposition_eq_swap, Equiv.symm_swap]

/-- An adjacent transposition is an involution. -/
@[simp]
theorem transposition_mul_self (i : Fin (n - 1)) :
    transposition i * transposition i = 1 :=
  Equiv.swap_mul_self _ _

/-- Right multiplication by an adjacent transposition is an involution. -/
@[simp]
theorem mul_transposition_mul_self (σ : Equiv.Perm (Fin n)) (i : Fin (n - 1)) :
    σ * transposition i * transposition i = σ :=
  Equiv.mul_swap_mul_self _ _ _

/-- `Fin.revPerm` intertwines each adjacent transposition with the reversed one. -/
theorem revPerm_mul_transposition (i : Fin (n - 1)) :
    Fin.revPerm * transposition i = transposition i.rev * Fin.revPerm := by
  rw [transposition_eq_swap, Equiv.mul_swap_eq_swap_mul, Fin.revPerm_apply, Fin.revPerm_apply,
    Equiv.swap_comm, ← strand_rev, ← strandSucc_rev, ← transposition_eq_swap]

/-- Conjugation by `Fin.revPerm` reverses the index of an adjacent transposition. -/
@[simp]
theorem revPerm_mul_transposition_mul_revPerm (i : Fin (n - 1)) :
    Fin.revPerm * transposition i * Fin.revPerm = transposition i.rev := by
  have hself : (Fin.revPerm : Equiv.Perm (Fin n)) * Fin.revPerm = 1 := by
    ext x
    simp
  rw [revPerm_mul_transposition, mul_assoc, hself, mul_one]

/-- Distinct braid indices have distinct adjacent transpositions. -/
theorem transposition_injective : Function.Injective (transposition (n := n)) := by
  intro i j hij
  have := congrArg (fun σ : Equiv.Perm (Fin n) ↦ (σ (strand i) : ℕ)) hij
  simp only [val_transposition_apply, val_strand] at this
  ext
  split_ifs at this <;> omega

/-- An adjacent transposition preserves the relative order of every pair of strands except the
two strands it swaps. -/
theorem eq_strand_of_transposition_lt {i : Fin (n - 1)} {x y : Fin n} (hxy : x < y)
    (h : transposition i y < transposition i x) : x = strand i ∧ y = strandSucc i := by
  rw [Fin.lt_def] at hxy h
  simp only [val_transposition_apply] at h
  simp only [Fin.ext_iff, val_strand, val_strandSucc]
  split_ifs at h <;> omega

/-- The transposition underlying an elementary braid is Mathlib's adjacent-swap generator. -/
theorem transposition_eq_swap_castSucc_succ {m : ℕ} (i : Fin m) :
    transposition (n := m + 1) i = Equiv.swap i.castSucc i.succ :=
  by
    rw [transposition_eq_swap]
    have hstrand : strand (n := m + 1) i = i.castSucc := Fin.ext (by simp)
    have hstrandSucc : strandSucc (n := m + 1) i = i.succ := Fin.ext (by simp)
    rw [hstrand, hstrandSucc]

/-- The adjacent transpositions underlying disjoint elementary braids commute. -/
theorem transposition_mul_transposition_comm {i j : Fin (n - 1)}
    (h : (i : ℕ) + 2 ≤ j ∨ (j : ℕ) + 2 ≤ i) :
    transposition i * transposition j = transposition j * transposition i :=
  (Equiv.Perm.disjoint_swap_swap (nodup_strand_strandSucc_strand_strandSucc h)).commute.eq

/-- The adjacent transpositions underlying neighbouring elementary braids satisfy the braid
relation. -/
theorem transposition_braid {i j : Fin (n - 1)}
    (h : (i : ℕ) + 1 = j ∨ (j : ℕ) + 1 = i) :
    transposition i * transposition j * transposition i =
      transposition j * transposition i * transposition j := by
  suffices hforward : ∀ {i j : Fin (n - 1)}, (i : ℕ) + 1 = j →
      transposition i * transposition j * transposition i =
        transposition j * transposition i * transposition j by
    rcases h with h | h
    · exact hforward h
    · exact (hforward h).symm
  intro i j h
  have hij : i ≠ j := fun hij ↦ by
    have := congrArg Fin.val hij
    omega
  have hji := strand_eq_strandSucc_of_succ h
  have hab := strand_ne_strandSucc i
  have hcb := (strandSucc_ne_strandSucc hij).symm
  rw [transposition_eq_swap, transposition_eq_swap, hji]
  exact swap_braid hab hcb

/-- The underlying permutation of the strands of a braid, obtained from the universal property
`TauCeti.BraidGroup.lift` and the two relations satisfied by the adjacent transpositions. -/
def permHom (n : ℕ) : BraidGroup n →* Equiv.Perm (Fin n) :=
  lift transposition transposition_mul_transposition_comm transposition_braid

@[simp]
theorem permHom_sigma (i : Fin (n - 1)) : permHom n (sigma i) = transposition i := by
  rw [permHom, lift_sigma]

/-- Every permutation of the strands is realised by a braid. -/
theorem permHom_surjective (n : ℕ) : Function.Surjective (permHom n) := by
  cases n with
  | zero => exact fun σ ↦ ⟨1, by ext x; exact x.elim0⟩
  | succ m =>
    have htop : Subgroup.closure (Set.range fun i : Fin m ↦ Equiv.swap i.castSucc i.succ) = ⊤ :=
      Subgroup.closure_eq_top_of_mclosure_eq_top (Equiv.Perm.mclosure_swap_castSucc_succ m)
    rw [← MonoidHom.range_eq_top, eq_top_iff, ← htop, Subgroup.closure_le]
    rintro _ ⟨i, rfl⟩
    exact ⟨sigma i, by rw [permHom_sigma, transposition_eq_swap_castSucc_succ]⟩

/-- The homomorphism from `BraidGroup (n + 1)` to `BraidGroup (n + 2)` that adds one strand and
leaves it uncrossed: each elementary braid is sent to the elementary braid of the same index, and
the new top strand is never touched. This is the homomorphism the Markov stabilization move is
built from. -/
def strandIncl {n : ℕ} : BraidGroup (n + 1) →* BraidGroup (n + 2) :=
  lift (fun i : Fin n ↦ sigma i.castSucc) (fun h ↦ sigma_mul_sigma_comm (by simpa using h))
    (fun h ↦ sigma_braid (by simpa using h))

@[simp]
theorem strandIncl_sigma {n : ℕ} (i : Fin n) : strandIncl (sigma i) = sigma i.castSucc := by
  rw [strandIncl, lift_sigma]

/-- Viewing a generator index one braid group up, as `TauCeti.BraidGroup.strandIncl` does, keeps
the lower strand it crosses. -/
theorem strand_castSucc {n : ℕ} (i : Fin (n + 1 - 1)) :
    strand (n := n + 2) (i.castSucc : Fin (n + 1)) = (strand i).castSucc :=
  Fin.ext (by simp)

/-- Viewing a generator index one braid group up, as `TauCeti.BraidGroup.strandIncl` does, keeps
the upper strand it crosses. -/
theorem strandSucc_castSucc {n : ℕ} (i : Fin (n + 1 - 1)) :
    strandSucc (n := n + 2) (i.castSucc : Fin (n + 1)) = (strandSucc i).castSucc :=
  Fin.ext (by simp)

/-- Adding an uncrossed strand does not change the exponent sum of a braid.

Not `@[simp]`: the `BraidGroup` abbreviation puts its Coxeter index in the form `n + 2 - 1`,
which `simp` normalizes to `n + 1`, so the left-hand side is not in simp normal form. -/
theorem exponentSum_strandIncl {n : ℕ} (b : BraidGroup (n + 1)) :
    ArtinGroup.exponentSum _ (strandIncl b) = ArtinGroup.exponentSum _ b := by
  have h : (ArtinGroup.exponentSum (CoxeterMatrix.A (n + 2 - 1))).comp strandIncl =
      ArtinGroup.exponentSum (CoxeterMatrix.A (n + 1 - 1)) :=
    hom_ext fun i ↦ by
      rw [MonoidHom.comp_apply, strandIncl_sigma i, exponentSum_sigma, exponentSum_sigma]
  exact DFunLike.congr_fun h b

/-- The exponent parity of an elementary braid is the nonidentity element. -/
@[simp]
theorem exponentParity_sigma (i : Fin (n - 1)) :
    ArtinGroup.exponentParity (CoxeterMatrix.A (n - 1)) (sigma i) = Multiplicative.ofAdd 1 := by
  rw [sigma_eq_gen, ArtinGroup.exponentParity_gen]

/-- Adding an uncrossed strand does not change the exponent parity of a braid.

Not `@[simp]`, for the reason given at `TauCeti.BraidGroup.exponentSum_strandIncl`. -/
theorem exponentParity_strandIncl {n : ℕ} (b : BraidGroup (n + 1)) :
    ArtinGroup.exponentParity _ (strandIncl b) = ArtinGroup.exponentParity _ b := by
  rw [ArtinGroup.exponentParity_apply, ArtinGroup.exponentParity_apply, exponentSum_strandIncl]

/-- **The sign of the strand permutation is the exponent sum modulo `2`**: the sign of the
permutation of a braid is `(-1)` to its exponent sum. -/
theorem sign_permHom (b : BraidGroup n) :
    Equiv.Perm.sign (permHom n b) =
      (-1) ^ (ArtinGroup.exponentSum (CoxeterMatrix.A (n - 1)) b).toAdd := by
  have h : Equiv.Perm.sign.comp (permHom n) =
      (zpowersHom ℤˣ (-1)).comp (ArtinGroup.exponentSum (CoxeterMatrix.A (n - 1))) :=
    hom_ext fun i ↦ by
      simp [transposition_eq_swap, Equiv.Perm.sign_swap (strand_ne_strandSucc i)]
  simpa only [MonoidHom.comp_apply, zpowersHom_apply] using DFunLike.congr_fun h b

/-- A braid has an even strand permutation exactly when its exponent parity is trivial. -/
theorem sign_permHom_eq_one_iff (b : BraidGroup n) :
    Equiv.Perm.sign (permHom n b) = 1 ↔
      ArtinGroup.exponentParity (CoxeterMatrix.A (n - 1)) b = 1 := by
  rw [sign_permHom, ArtinGroup.exponentParity_eq_one_iff]
  rcases Int.even_or_odd (ArtinGroup.exponentSum (CoxeterMatrix.A (n - 1)) b).toAdd with h | h
  · simp [h, h.neg_one_zpow]
  · simp [h.neg_one_zpow, Int.not_even_iff_odd.2 h]

/-- From two strands on, the exponent parity is onto the group of order two. -/
theorem exponentParity_surjective (hn : 2 ≤ n) :
    Function.Surjective (ArtinGroup.exponentParity (CoxeterMatrix.A (n - 1))) := by
  have : Nonempty (Fin (n - 1)) := ⟨⟨0, by omega⟩⟩
  exact ArtinGroup.exponentParity_surjective _

/-- From two strands on, the braids of trivial exponent parity form a subgroup of index two. -/
theorem index_ker_exponentParity (hn : 2 ≤ n) :
    (ArtinGroup.exponentParity (CoxeterMatrix.A (n - 1))).ker.index = 2 := by
  have : Nonempty (Fin (n - 1)) := ⟨⟨0, by omega⟩⟩
  exact ArtinGroup.index_ker_exponentParity _

/-- **The braid group on two strands is infinite cyclic**: the exponent sum identifies it with
`ℤ`. -/
theorem exponentSum_bijective_two :
    Function.Bijective (ArtinGroup.exponentSum (CoxeterMatrix.A (2 - 1))) := by
  have : Subsingleton (Fin (2 - 1)) := inferInstanceAs (Subsingleton (Fin 1))
  exact ArtinGroup.exponentSum_bijective _

/-- The added strand does not interfere: on the old strands, `strandIncl b` permutes exactly as
`b` does. -/
@[simp]
theorem permHom_strandIncl_castSucc {n : ℕ} (b : BraidGroup (n + 1)) (i : Fin (n + 1)) :
    permHom (n + 2) (strandIncl b) i.castSucc = (permHom (n + 1) b i).castSucc := by
  revert i
  induction b using sigma_induction_on with
  | sigma k =>
    intro i
    rw [strandIncl_sigma, permHom_sigma, permHom_sigma, transposition_eq_swap_castSucc_succ,
      transposition_eq_swap_castSucc_succ, Fin.succ_castSucc]
    exact ((Fin.castSucc_injective (n + 1)).map_swap _ _ _).symm
  | one => simp
  | mul b b' hb hb' =>
    intro i
    rw [map_mul, map_mul, map_mul, Equiv.Perm.mul_apply, Equiv.Perm.mul_apply, hb' i,
      hb (permHom (n + 1) b' i)]
  | inv b hb =>
    intro i
    rw [map_inv, map_inv, map_inv]
    refine (permHom (n + 2) (strandIncl b)).injective ?_
    rw [hb ((permHom (n + 1) b)⁻¹ i)]
    simp

/-- The last elementary braid of `BraidGroup (n + 2)` transposes the last two strands. -/
theorem permHom_sigma_last (n : ℕ) :
    permHom (n + 2) (sigma (Fin.last n)) =
      Equiv.swap (Fin.castSucc (Fin.last n)) (Fin.last (n + 1)) := by
  rw [permHom_sigma, transposition_eq_swap_castSucc_succ, Fin.succ_last]

/-- The strand added by `strandIncl` is left where it is. -/
@[simp]
theorem permHom_strandIncl_last {n : ℕ} (b : BraidGroup (n + 1)) :
    permHom (n + 2) (strandIncl b) (Fin.last (n + 1)) = Fin.last (n + 1) := by
  induction b using sigma_induction_on with
  | sigma k =>
    rw [strandIncl_sigma, permHom_sigma, transposition_eq_swap_castSucc_succ,
      Fin.succ_castSucc]
    exact Equiv.swap_apply_of_ne_of_ne (Fin.castSucc_ne_last _).symm (Fin.castSucc_ne_last _).symm
  | one => simp
  | mul b b' hb hb' => rw [map_mul, map_mul, Equiv.Perm.mul_apply, hb', hb]
  | inv b hb =>
    rw [map_inv, map_inv]
    refine (permHom (n + 2) (strandIncl b)).injective ?_
    rw [hb]
    simp

/-- Adding an uncrossed strand to a braid gives the trivial permutation exactly when the braid
already permuted its strands trivially. -/
@[simp]
theorem permHom_strandIncl_eq_one_iff {n : ℕ} {b : BraidGroup (n + 1)} :
    permHom (n + 2) (strandIncl b) = 1 ↔ permHom (n + 1) b = 1 := by
  refine ⟨fun h ↦ Equiv.ext fun i ↦ ?_, fun h ↦ Equiv.ext fun i ↦ ?_⟩
  · have hi := DFunLike.congr_fun h i.castSucc
    rwa [permHom_strandIncl_castSucc, Equiv.Perm.one_apply, Fin.castSucc_inj] at hi
  · induction i using Fin.lastCases with
    | last => rw [permHom_strandIncl_last, Equiv.Perm.one_apply]
    | cast i => rw [permHom_strandIncl_castSucc, h, Equiv.Perm.one_apply, Equiv.Perm.one_apply]

/-- A braid on the first `n + 1` strands, with two uncrossed strands added above it, commutes with
the crossing of those two new strands. -/
theorem commute_sigma_last_strandIncl_strandIncl {n : ℕ} (d : BraidGroup (n + 1)) :
    Commute (sigma (n := n + 3) (Fin.last (n + 1))) (strandIncl (strandIncl d)) := by
  induction d using sigma_induction_on with
  | sigma j =>
    rw [strandIncl_sigma, strandIncl_sigma]
    refine sigma_mul_sigma_comm (Or.inr ?_)
    simp only [Fin.val_castSucc, Fin.val_last]
    have := j.isLt
    omega
  | one => simp
  | mul d d' hd hd' => rw [map_mul, map_mul]; exact hd.mul_right hd'
  | inv d hd => rw [map_inv, map_inv]; exact hd.inv_right

/-- The homomorphism from `BraidGroup (n + 1)` to `BraidGroup (n + m + 1)` that places a braid on
the bottom `n + 1` strands and adds `m` uncrossed strands above it: each elementary braid keeps its
index. For `m = 1` this is `TauCeti.BraidGroup.strandIncl` (`TauCeti.BraidGroup.castAdd_one`). -/
def castAdd {n : ℕ} (m : ℕ) : BraidGroup (n + 1) →* BraidGroup (n + m + 1) :=
  lift (fun i : Fin n ↦ sigma (Fin.castAdd m i)) (fun h ↦ sigma_mul_sigma_comm (by simpa using h))
    (fun h ↦ sigma_braid (by simpa using h))

@[simp]
theorem castAdd_sigma {n : ℕ} (m : ℕ) (i : Fin n) :
    castAdd m (sigma i) = sigma (Fin.castAdd m i) := by
  rw [castAdd, lift_sigma]

/-- Adding one uncrossed strand on top is `TauCeti.BraidGroup.strandIncl`. -/
@[simp]
theorem castAdd_one {n : ℕ} : castAdd (n := n) 1 = strandIncl :=
  hom_ext fun i ↦ by rw [castAdd_sigma, strandIncl_sigma]; rfl

/-- The homomorphism from `BraidGroup (m + 1)` to `BraidGroup (n + m + 1)` that places a braid on
the top `m + 1` strands and adds `n` uncrossed strands below it: each elementary braid is moved up
by `n`. -/
def natAdd (n : ℕ) {m : ℕ} : BraidGroup (m + 1) →* BraidGroup (n + m + 1) :=
  lift (fun i : Fin m ↦ sigma (Fin.natAdd n i)) (fun h ↦ sigma_mul_sigma_comm (by simp; omega))
    (fun h ↦ sigma_braid (by simp; omega))

@[simp]
theorem natAdd_sigma (n : ℕ) {m : ℕ} (i : Fin m) :
    natAdd n (sigma i) = sigma (Fin.natAdd n i) := by
  rw [natAdd, lift_sigma]

/-- The pure braid group: the braids that return every strand to its own position. -/
def pureSubgroup (n : ℕ) : Subgroup (BraidGroup n) := (permHom n).ker

/-- The defining equation of `TauCeti.BraidGroup.pureSubgroup`. -/
theorem pureSubgroup_eq_ker (n : ℕ) : pureSubgroup n = (permHom n).ker :=
  (rfl)

@[simp]
theorem mem_pureSubgroup {b : BraidGroup n} : b ∈ pureSubgroup n ↔ permHom n b = 1 := Iff.rfl

instance : (pureSubgroup n).Normal := (permHom n).normal_ker

/-- The pure braid group has index `n !`, the order of the symmetric group on the strands. -/
@[simp]
theorem index_pureSubgroup (n : ℕ) : (pureSubgroup n).index = n.factorial := by
  rw [pureSubgroup_eq_ker, Subgroup.index_ker, MonoidHom.range_eq_top.2 (permHom_surjective n),
    Subgroup.card_top, Nat.card_perm, Nat.card_fin]

instance : (pureSubgroup n).FiniteIndex :=
  ⟨by rw [index_pureSubgroup]; exact n.factorial_ne_zero⟩

/-- The braid group modulo the pure braids is the symmetric group on the strands, by the strand
permutation. -/
noncomputable def quotientPureSubgroupEquiv (n : ℕ) :
    BraidGroup n ⧸ pureSubgroup n ≃* Equiv.Perm (Fin n) :=
  (QuotientGroup.quotientMulEquivOfEq (pureSubgroup_eq_ker n)).trans
    (QuotientGroup.quotientKerEquivOfSurjective _ (permHom_surjective n))

/-- The isomorphism `TauCeti.BraidGroup.quotientPureSubgroupEquiv` sends the class of a braid to
its strand permutation. -/
@[simp]
theorem quotientPureSubgroupEquiv_mk (b : BraidGroup n) :
    quotientPureSubgroupEquiv n b = permHom n b := by
  rw [quotientPureSubgroupEquiv, MulEquiv.trans_apply, QuotientGroup.quotientMulEquivOfEq_mk,
    QuotientGroup.quotientKerEquivOfSurjective_apply_mk]

/-- **Pure braids are compatible with strand inclusion.** A braid is pure exactly when it stays
pure after adding an uncrossed strand. -/
theorem strandIncl_mem_pureSubgroup {n : ℕ} {b : BraidGroup (n + 1)} :
    strandIncl b ∈ pureSubgroup (n + 2) ↔ b ∈ pureSubgroup (n + 1) := by
  rw [mem_pureSubgroup, mem_pureSubgroup, permHom_strandIncl_eq_one_iff]

/-- The pure braids on `n + 1` strands are those which stay pure after adding an uncrossed
strand. -/
@[simp]
theorem comap_strandIncl_pureSubgroup (n : ℕ) :
    (pureSubgroup (n + 2)).comap strandIncl = pureSubgroup (n + 1) :=
  Subgroup.ext fun _ ↦ strandIncl_mem_pureSubgroup

/-- An integer power of an elementary braid is pure exactly when its exponent is even. -/
theorem sigma_zpow_mem_pureSubgroup_iff (i : Fin (n - 1)) (k : ℤ) :
    sigma i ^ k ∈ pureSubgroup n ↔ Even k := by
  have horder : orderOf (transposition i) = 2 := by
    rw [transposition_eq_swap]
    exact (Equiv.Perm.swap_isSwap_iff.mpr (strand_ne_strandSucc i)).orderOf
  rw [mem_pureSubgroup, map_zpow, permHom_sigma, ← orderOf_dvd_iff_zpow_eq_one, horder]
  exact even_iff_two_dvd.symm

/-- A natural-number power of an elementary braid is pure exactly when its exponent is even. -/
theorem sigma_pow_mem_pureSubgroup_iff (i : Fin (n - 1)) (k : ℕ) :
    sigma i ^ k ∈ pureSubgroup n ↔ Even k := by
  simpa only [zpow_natCast, Int.even_natCast] using
    sigma_zpow_mem_pureSubgroup_iff i (k : ℤ)

/-- The square of an elementary braid is a pure braid: the two strands cross twice and end where
they started. -/
theorem sigma_sq_mem_pureSubgroup (i : Fin (n - 1)) : sigma i ^ 2 ∈ pureSubgroup n := by
  rw [sigma_pow_mem_pureSubgroup_iff]
  exact even_two

/-- An elementary braid is not pure: it exchanges the two strands that it crosses. -/
theorem sigma_not_mem_pureSubgroup (i : Fin (n - 1)) : sigma i ∉ pureSubgroup n := by
  intro h
  have heven := (sigma_pow_mem_pureSubgroup_iff i 1).mp (by simpa using h)
  simp [even_iff_two_dvd] at heven

/-- Two elementary braids sharing a strand do not commute. -/
theorem not_commute_sigma {i j : Fin (n - 1)}
    (h : (i : ℕ) + 1 = j ∨ (j : ℕ) + 1 = i) :
    ¬ Commute (sigma i) (sigma j) := by
  suffices hforward : ∀ {i j : Fin (n - 1)}, (i : ℕ) + 1 = j →
      ¬ Commute (sigma i) (sigma j) by
    rcases h with h | h
    · exact hforward h
    · exact fun hcomm ↦ hforward h hcomm.symm
  intro i j h
  have hij : i ≠ j := fun hij ↦ by
    have := congrArg Fin.val hij
    omega
  have hji := strand_eq_strandSucc_of_succ h
  have hab := strand_ne_strandSucc i
  have hac := strand_ne_strandSucc_of_succ h
  have hbc := strandSucc_ne_strandSucc hij
  intro hcomm
  have h₁ := congrArg (permHom n) hcomm.eq
  rw [map_mul, map_mul, permHom_sigma, permHom_sigma, transposition_eq_swap,
    transposition_eq_swap, hji] at h₁
  have h₂ := congrArg (fun σ : Equiv.Perm (Fin n) ↦ σ (strand i)) h₁
  simp only [Equiv.Perm.mul_apply, Equiv.swap_apply_of_ne_of_ne hab hac,
    Equiv.swap_apply_left] at h₂
  exact hbc h₂

/-- The braid group on at least three strands is nonabelian: the first two elementary braids
share a strand, so they do not commute. -/
theorem exists_not_commute (h : 3 ≤ n) :
    ∃ a b : BraidGroup n, ¬ Commute a b :=
  ⟨sigma ⟨0, by omega⟩, sigma ⟨1, by omega⟩, not_commute_sigma (Or.inl rfl)⟩

end BraidGroup

end TauCeti
